This workspace holds a C++ Snake whose headless mode is complete and correct, with the terminal front end removed. `src/tui.hpp`'s `play()` returns immediately, so `./snake` with no arguments does nothing at all.

Implement the terminal game per SPEC.md section 10. It is driven under a real pseudo-terminal by the graded run, so each of these is something a driver watches:

- the alternate screen entered with `ESC[?1049h` on start and left with `ESC[?1049l` on every exit path, with raw mode undone the same way;
- the whole playfield drawn with the same four glyphs the summary uses, so the drawn board and the summary's `board` string agree cell for cell;
- a status line containing `Score:` and the score, plus `PAUSED`, `GAME OVER` and `YOU WIN` when each applies;
- the arrow keys and `wasd` turning under the same rule the rules module enforces, `p` pausing, `r` restarting, `q` quitting with status 0;
- `--tick-ms <n>`, where 0 means the game advances only on input;
- a terminal too small getting a message naming `terminal` and NO broken frame, and a board once it grows.

How you draw is yours: raw escape sequences or the system ncurses both satisfy this. Change nothing outside the terminal front end.

The machine has Apple Clang with C++23, CMake, Ninja and the system ncurses (`-lncurses`, `<curses.h>`). There is no network, so a dependency fetched at build time will fail the build.

`tools/run_tests.sh` builds and then runs every executable directly under `tests/`; it fails when there are no tests at all. The graded run executes the built binary and never reads your source.
